  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Strong to severe showers and thunderstorms today \nwith additional chances for daily thunderstorms through mid \nweek.\n\n12z RNK soundings showed fairly unstable airmass with SBCAPE of\nover 1300 J/kg. PWATS are running a little lower (1.29\")\ncompared to model/mesoscale analysis.\n\nStill thinking strong/severe storms will be more of a concern\nthis afternoon/evening than flooding as storm motion will run\n15-25 mph. Some training possible.\n\nStill looking at moderately to strongly unstable airmass later \ntoday (1000-3000 J/kg MLCAPE). Severe parameters point to the\ncentral VA Piedmont into the Shenandoah Valley as having better\nthreat of damaging winds compared to the southwest VA/NW NC\nmountains.\n\nPrior to the storms, temperatures should reach the lower to mid\n90s east of the mountains, with 80s in the west. \n\nAny smoke/haze should erode or shift east once the storms \ndevelop and mix out the particulates.\n\nPrevious discussion...\n\nRidging aloft and at the surface continued to weaken as\ntroughing gradually replaced it aloft. Currently a short wave \ntraveling clockwise around the Bermuda high pressure center was \nsustaining relatively strong storms near the VA/TN border. A few\nof the smaller cells had worked their way into the western \nmountains. CAMs show this activity moving into SE WV eventually \nand weakening towards morning. \n\nIncreasing cyclonic flow aloft along with lee troughing will\ninteract with strong heating and dew points in the upper 60s/ \nlower 70s to produce more showers and thunderstorms, especially \nfrom noon on into the evening. Given the temperatures and\nmoisture, instability will be moderate, however shear will be\nrelatively weak and storm mode is expected to be clusters and a \nfew broken line segments moving east-southeast around 20 kts. \nConvection continues off and on through the overnight hours at \nwhich point a cold front finally moves into the region. This \nfeature along with lee troughing will foster additional\nconvection Sunday. As drier air works in from the north,\nconvection will sink south. For both days, but especially\nSaturday given the upper level support, numerous storms may\nbecome strong to severe, with downbursts the main concern. \nHeavy rain leading to flash flooding will also be possible.\n\nMonday through Wednesday we will see additional chances for  \nafternoon showers and storms, with another front likely pushing \nthrough Wednesday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...One more day of very warm weather, then\ntemperatures begin to moderate. Smoke also lessens beginning \ntoday. \n\nStill may see smoke around from Canadian wildfires today, but\nthis will move out of the area by this evening as the upper \nflow shifts. Temperatures too will be cooler after one more day \nof 90+ degree heat along and east of the Blue Ridge. Most days \nthis week will be in the 70s and 80s for the mountains and the \n80s or low 90s for the Piedmont (after today). Heat index values\nstay shy of advisory criteria once again today.",
